cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  decision-making-skills: agent skills for decisions under uncertainty, with an
  evaluation harness that measures whether they work
message: >-
  Cite the software entry below. The published results measure whether a skill
  fires when it should; decision quality is not yet measured, and no skill in
  this repository carries a verdict. SCORECARD.md holds the verdicts and is
  empty. `paper/` is a draft, so there is no paper to cite.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Campa
    given-names: Angel
    alias: AngelCampa1
repository-code: https://github.com/AngelCampa1/decision-making-skills
url: https://angelcampa1.github.io/decision-making-skills/
abstract: >-
  A markdown Agent Skill that routes a decision to one of six procedures:
  evidence ledger, switching conditions, consequence cascade, decide or wait,
  council, and ask or decide. It reads only the one it routes to.
  Beside it is a Python evaluation harness (`decision_evals`) that measures
  whether such a skill changes behaviour. Runs are pre-registered, and the
  harness refuses to publish a result whose prediction cannot be shown by git
  ancestry to predate its data. The four-arm design is written and enforced,
  and it has not run: a confirmation run compares a skill against four arms on
  the same items (off, on, a token- and structure-matched placebo, and plain
  chain-of-thought), so that a skill beating nothing while matching filler is
  reported as a length effect. `check_placebo_match` refuses a placebo of the
  wrong size or shape on every local gate run, and no published run has used
  the placebo or chain-of-thought arm. Labels are re-derived blind by a panel
  of independent model instances, and the panel's agreement is reported
  chance-corrected (Fleiss' kappa, Krippendorff's alpha) alongside the raw
  rate. Statistics are exact and resampling-based, with templates as the
  resampling unit, and calibration goes through the Murphy decomposition.
  Published results to date measure skill *triggering*, whether a skill fires
  when it should. Decision quality is not yet measured.
